    {
      "id": "tyy438x",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Latitudes"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Latitudes are parallel horizontal circles measuring angular distance North or South of the Equator (0°).",
        "Longitudes are non-parallel vertical semi-circles running from the North Pole to the South Pole, meeting at both poles.",
        "Indian Standard Time (IST) is calculated based on the 82.5° E longitude passing through Mirzapur, Uttar Pradesh.",
        "The Tropic of Cancer (23.5° N) divides India into almost two equal geographic halves.",
        "Every 1° change in longitude corresponds to a time difference of exactly 4 minutes."
      ],
      "id": "26rzk8g"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dum43aj",
      "content": "• Latitudes are imaginary lines drawn parallel to the Equator. "
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4vv6sak",
      "content": "• They measure distance north or south of the Equator."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "z5hijit",
      "content": "• The Equator is 0° latitude."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ydja6ei",
      "content": "• Latitudes extend from 0° to 90° North and 0° to 90° South."
    },
    {
      "id": "mtqeadi",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Important Latitudes"
