    {
      "id": "oh0pepz",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Longitudes"
    },
    {
      "id": "iwwdh85",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "4uyiadu",
      "src": "/books/Guide/Cluster_035_GS & GK GUIDE BOOK/images/image_4uyiadu.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "m5ua85r",
      "content": "<img></img>• Longitudes are imaginary vertical lines joining North and South poles."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "us88mml",
      "content": "• They measure distance east or west of the Prime Meridian."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "g4v7if5",
      "content": "• The Prime Meridian is located at Greenwich, London."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wgk9ds2",
      "content": "• There are 360° of longitude."
    },
    {
      "id": "rfpjfiw",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Indian Standard Time (IST)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "u47d7bd",
      "content": "• India follows Indian Standard Time."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vdfe9vp",
      "content": "• The standard meridian is 82°30′ East longitude.       "
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vff3zuj",
      "content": "• It passes near Mirzapur in Uttar Pradesh."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "g1jj3l7",
      "content": "• IST is 5 hours 30 minutes ahead of GMT."
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "The distance between two consecutive latitudes is approximately 111 km.",
      "id": "8nwqeqx"
    },
